# Migrating `BackgroundProcessor` to .NET 8

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** — removed entirely in .NET 5+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is gone** — no equivalent needed since abort can't happen
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** — catch block is dead code

## Modern .NET 8 Implementation

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IAsyncDisposable
{
    private Task? _workerTask;
    private CancellationTokenSource? _cts;

    public void Start()
    {
        _cts = new CancellationTokenSource();
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token); // pass token to unblock
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break;
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        if (_cts is null || _workerTask is null)
            return;

        _cts.Cancel();

        // Wait for graceful exit with a timeout
        var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5);
        if (await Task.WhenAny(_workerTask, Task.Delay(effectiveTimeout)) != _workerTask)
        {
            // Worker didn't stop in time — log a warning.
            // There is NO equivalent to Abort() in .NET 8.
        }
    }

    public async ValueTask DisposeAsync()
    {
        await StopAsync();
        _cts?.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **No forced kill** | .NET 8 has no `Thread.Abort`. If `GetNextItem` blocks forever without respecting the token, the thread leaks. You *must* make blocking calls cancellation-aware. |
| **Cooperative cancellation** | Every long/blocking call needs to accept `CancellationToken`. If wrapping legacy sync code, use `Task.Run` + token checks, not `Thread.Interrupt()` (also unreliable). |
| **`Thread.Sleep` → `Task.Delay`** | Avoid blocking a thread pool thread; use `await Task.Delay(duration, token)`. |
| **`volatile bool` → `CancellationToken`** | The token is thread-safe, composable, and integrates with async APIs. |
| **Exception semantics differ** | `OperationCanceledException` is *not* auto-rethrown like `ThreadAbortException` was — no `ResetAbort` equivalent needed. |
| **Dispose pattern** | Implement `IAsyncDisposable` so callers in DI containers or `await using` blocks get clean shutdown. |

## If You Can't Make Blocking Calls Cancellable

As a last resort, isolate truly uncooperative code on a dedicated thread:

```csharp
// Dedicated thread that can be abandoned (not aborted) on timeout
_workerThread = new Thread(() =>
{
    try { ProcessLoop(_cts.Token); }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) { }
});
_workerThread.IsBackground = true; // dies with process
_workerThread.Start();
```

Setting `IsBackground = true` ensures the thread won't keep the process alive on shutdown — but it gets no cleanup opportunity. Prefer making code cancellation-aware instead.
